```{r setup, include = FALSE}
stopifnot(require(knitr))
# Nothing here is evaluated. Every chunk is code the reader runs on their own
# machine, and evaluating any of it records the build machine's paths and
# compiler output. Set eval here so that a chunk added later inherits it.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  eval = FALSE
)
```

[e1]: https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html

`bayesnec` fits its models with `brms` [@Burkner2017; @Burkner2018], which
passes them to Stan (<https://mc-stan.org/>). Stan does not interpret a model;
instead, each model is translated into C++ and compiled into a program, and that
program is run. A C++ toolchain is therefore required to fit any model at all,
and compilation is also the reason the first fit of a session takes longer than
the fits after it.

`brms` samples through either of two back ends (i.e. the R packages through
which it compiles and runs the Stan program). `rstan` [@stan2020] is installed
as a dependency of `brms` whether or not it is used as the sampler, so it is
present on any machine that can load `brms`. However, this package's own
vignettes are built with `cmdstanr`, which is not on CRAN and is not in the
dependency list of `bayesnec`, and which is selected with
`options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")`. We describe the installation of
`cmdstanr` below, and the `rstan` route is documented by the Stan project
(<https://github.com/stan-dev/rstan/wiki/RStan-Getting-Started>).

# Software requirements

| Software | Purpose |
|---|---|
| R | the language the package runs in |
| A C++ toolchain | compiles each model before it is fitted |
| CmdStan | the sampler that runs the compiled model |

Of these, the toolchain is the component that differs between operating systems
(Rtools on Windows, the Xcode Command Line Tools on macOS, and the standard
build tools on Linux), and it is also the component that most often fails.

# Route by operating system

Windows requires the most care, and is described in its own section below.
Historically, the difficulties of running Stan on Windows came from `rstan`,
which requires matched versions of several compiled packages. `cmdstanr` avoids
most of these, and the difficulties that remain concern the toolchain and the
installation path.

Linux requires the least care, because the build tools of a current
distribution are already what Stan requires, and the default installation path
raises none of the problems described in the Windows section.

These instructions have not been verified on macOS, so for macOS we point to
the instructions maintained by the projects themselves.

Whether an installation works is determined by the check in the *Verification*
section, regardless of which of these routes was followed.

# Windows

## R and Rtools

Install R first (from <https://cran.r-project.org/bin/windows/base/>), and then
the Rtools that matches it (from
<https://cran.r-project.org/bin/windows/Rtools/>). Accept the default
installation location for each.

Rtools is versioned against the minor version of R, and CRAN's Rtools page lists
which Rtools goes with which R. A mismatch between them is the most common
single cause of a failed setup, so install R first and read that page, instead
of reusing an Rtools already on the machine.

`cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain()` names the Rtools version that `cmdstanr`
is looking for, and that is the version to install. The function becomes
available once `cmdstanr` has been installed (see the next section). Under
`cmdstanr` 0.9.0 and R 4.6.1, for example, the message is

```
Rtools45 was not found but is required to run CmdStan with R version 4.6.1.
Please install Rtools45 and run cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain().
```

The version `cmdstanr` looks for is fixed in the installed `cmdstanr` and is not
read from CRAN, so it may differ from the Rtools that CRAN pairs with that
version of R. That case is described under *Recurring problems on Windows*
below.

## R packages

`cmdstanr` is not on CRAN, so it is installed from the Stan project's own
repository:

```{r install_cmdstanr}
install.packages("cmdstanr",
                 repos = c("https://stan-dev.r-universe.dev",
                           getOption("repos")))
```

`bayesnec` and the remaining packages are installed from CRAN, and installing
`bayesnec` also installs `brms`:

```{r install_bayesnec}
install.packages("bayesnec")
```

## The CmdStan installation

Before installing CmdStan, confirm that the compiler is configured. On a current
Rtools this check passes without making any change, and `fix = TRUE` repairs the
problems it can, instead of only reporting them.

```{r toolchain_fix}
cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain(fix = TRUE)
```

Then install CmdStan with the code below, which sets three things, each of which
has been observed to stop a fresh installation on Windows.

```{r install_cmdstan}
dir.create("C:/cmdstan")           # install_cmdstan() does not create it
options(timeout = 1800)            # R's default of 60 seconds is too short
cmdstanr::install_cmdstan(dir = "C:/cmdstan")
```

The directory has to exist before `install_cmdstan()` is called, because the
function does not create it and instead stops with
`Assertion on 'dir' failed: Directory 'C:/cmdstan' does not exist.`

The download needs a longer timeout than R's default. The CmdStan archive is
about 49 MB, and its download is governed by `options(timeout)`, not by the
`timeout` argument of `install_cmdstan()`. When this was measured on a
connection delivering about 500 KB/s, the download failed with
`Download of CmdStan failed with error` at the default timeout, and completed in
4.9 minutes at `timeout = 1800` with nothing else changed. We recommend setting
the timeout in any case, because it has no effect where the download would have
completed anyway.

The installation path has to be a plain one. The default location is inside the
home directory, and on many Windows machines (particularly those managed by an
employer) that directory is synchronised by OneDrive, where the CmdStan build
fails. The build also fails where the path contains a space or a character
outside the standard English alphabet, as the home directory does when the
Windows user name contains one. `C:/cmdstan` avoids all three problems, although
installing there instead of in the default location requires two things in
return: the `CMDSTAN` variable described below, and `dir = "C:/cmdstan"` in any
later call to `install_cmdstan()`.

Because `C:/cmdstan` is not the default location, R has to be told where to find
it in each later session, and `cmdstanr` reads this setting from an environment
variable, not from an option. To set it, run `usethis::edit_r_environ()` (or
open `.Renviron` in your home directory), add the line below, and restart R.
Without this line, `cmdstanr` shows CmdStan as not installed in each new
session, and the path has to be set by hand in each one.

```
CMDSTAN=C:/cmdstan
```

R reads `.Renviron` only when it starts, which is why the edit takes effect in
the next session and not in the current one. The variable is the only setting
`cmdstanr` reads for this purpose: in `cmdstanr` 0.9.0,
`cmdstanr:::cmdstanr_initialize()` takes the path from `Sys.getenv("CMDSTAN")`
where that is set and from `~/.cmdstan` otherwise, and no option sets it.

The version number is not needed in the variable. Where `CMDSTAN` names a
directory that is not itself an installation (such as `C:/cmdstan`), `cmdstanr`
takes the newest `cmdstan-*` installation inside it, so the line above continues
to work after the next CmdStan upgrade without being edited. Setting the path by
hand, however, does need the version number, and this difference is a likely
source of error. `cmdstanr::set_cmdstan_path()` takes the path as given, so it
has to be called as `set_cmdstan_path("C:/cmdstan/cmdstan-2.39.0")`. Given the
parent directory instead, it sets the path to that directory, the version read
by `cmdstan_version()` is left at `NULL`, and it gives the warning

```
Can't find CmdStan makefile to detect version number. Path may not point to valid installation.
```

The back end is set by an option, not by an environment variable, so on all
platforms it belongs in `.Rprofile`, where it does not have to be set again in
each session:

```{r backend}
options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")
```

## Recurring problems on Windows

### A partly built CmdStan directory

Where `install_cmdstan()` fails, run it again with these arguments.

```{r reinstall}
cmdstanr::install_cmdstan(overwrite = TRUE, quiet = FALSE, dir = "C:/cmdstan")
```

Of these arguments, `overwrite = TRUE` is the important one. An attempt that
fails partway through the build results in a partly built directory, and a
second attempt without `overwrite` declines to replace it, giving an error that
resembles the first one. `quiet = FALSE` only prints more of the build output,
which is useful because the default output is truncated and does not identify
the stage that failed.

### Chains that do not start after an Rtools upgrade

A model compiles, but `cmdstanr` then gives the message
`No chains finished successfully`, which names no cause and no file. This
typically follows a change to Rtools after CmdStan was installed. CmdStan builds its
own `tbb.dll`, linked against the toolchain that built it, so a CmdStan built
under Rtools44 requires libraries that Rtools45 does not provide; the model
still compiles, but each chain then exits immediately with
`STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND`. Rebuilding CmdStan resolves the problem.

```{r rebuild}
cmdstanr::install_cmdstan(overwrite = TRUE, dir = "C:/cmdstan")
```

Anyone who upgrades R and Rtools after installing CmdStan will encounter this.

### The `cmdstanr` version and the Rtools it expects

Rtools is installed, but `cmdstanr` gives a message that it is missing. This is
because `cmdstanr` chooses which Rtools to look for from the R version, and that
choice is fixed in the installed `cmdstanr`. In 0.9.0,
`cmdstanr:::rtools4x_version()` returns Rtools44 for R 4.4.x and Rtools45 for
all versions of R from 4.5 onward, which is why R 4.6.1 is sent to Rtools45.
Version 0.8.0 sent all versions from R 4.4 onward to Rtools44, so an older
`cmdstanr` installed beside the Rtools that CRAN pairs with R 4.5.x searches for
a toolchain that was never installed.

Install `cmdstanr` 0.9.0 or later (as supplied by the r-universe repository
above), and install the Rtools named by `check_cmdstan_toolchain()` instead of
the one inferred from the R version.

### Slow compilation

Windows Defender inspects each newly compiled program, so a compile that would
otherwise take under a minute may take several. Where the machine's policy
allows, excluding the CmdStan directory from real-time scanning resolves this.

### Compilation blocked by policy

Some managed machines prevent a newly compiled program from running at all. The
block results from the machine's policy and not from a problem in the
configuration. It cannot be worked around from within R, and resolving it
requires whoever administers the machine.

# macOS

These steps have not been verified on macOS. They point to the upstream
instructions maintained by the projects themselves, and whether the installation
works is established by the check in the *Verification* section.

1. Install R from <https://cran.r-project.org/bin/macosx/>, choosing the build
   for your processor (the Apple Silicon and Intel builds differ).
2. Install the Xcode Command Line Tools by running `xcode-select --install` in
   Terminal. Some packages also require `gfortran`, and the current requirement
   is given on CRAN's macOS tools page.
3. Install the R packages as described in the Windows section above.
4. Follow the CmdStanR getting-started vignette
   (<https://mc-stan.org/cmdstanr/articles/cmdstanr.html>) for
   `check_cmdstan_toolchain()` and `install_cmdstan()`. The default installation
   path causes no difficulty on macOS, so `dir` need not be set, and no
   `CMDSTAN` variable is needed either.
5. Put `options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")` in `.Rprofile`, as in the Windows
   section.

# Linux

Install R through the distribution's package manager or from CRAN, together with
the standard build tools (`build-essential` on Debian and Ubuntu, or the
equivalent development group on other distributions). After installing the R
packages as described in the Windows section, run
`cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain()` and then `cmdstanr::install_cmdstan()`. As
on macOS, the default path causes no difficulty, so no `CMDSTAN` variable is
needed, and `options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")` belongs in `.Rprofile` as it
does on Windows.

# Verification

The installation is verified with two checks. Run them in order, because the
first inspects the toolchain and the second uses it.

```{r verify_toolchain}
cmdstanr::check_cmdstan_toolchain()
cmdstanr::cmdstan_version()
```

On a configured toolchain, `check_cmdstan_toolchain()` gives the message

```
The C++ toolchain required for CmdStan is setup properly!
```

and `cmdstan_version()` returns the version string of the installation that R is
using. An error from either function indicates a problem with the toolchain or
the path, and nothing below will work until it is resolved.

The second check fits a model. It is the decisive check, because it exercises
the whole path (`bnec()` calls `brms::brm()`, `brm()` writes a Stan program,
CmdStan compiles it, and the compiled program samples).

```{r verify_fit}
library(bayesnec)
data(nec_data)

options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")
set.seed(333)
fit <- bnec(y ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param"), data = nec_data,
            family = Beta(link = "identity"), chains = 2, seed = 333)
summary(fit)
```

A successful run compiles one Stan program, prints the sampling progress of two
chains, and returns an object of class `bayesnecfit`. Its `summary()` shows the
four parameters of the **nec4param** equation (**bot**, **top**, **beta** and
**nec**), each with an `Rhat` near 1.00. These are followed by a *Further
Distributional Parameters* block containing `phi` (the precision of the Beta
response), then the *NEC* estimate with its credible interval, and then a
Bayesian R-squared. Two chains are enough to show that the toolchain works. An
analysis, however, should use the `brms` default of four chains, which the
other vignettes take.

Once this fit returns, the modelling vignettes can be followed, beginning with
[Single model usage][e1].

# Provenance

The Windows failures described above, and the measurements quoted with them (the
49 MB archive, the download that failed at R's default timeout and completed in
4.9 minutes at `timeout = 1800`, the OneDrive build failure, and the `tbb.dll`
linked against a superseded Rtools), are taken from the software setup module of
the concentration-response modelling course
(<https://github.com/open-AIMS/cr_modelling_training>, commit `9d05595`). That
module records what was observed on fresh Windows machines during 2026, and
these are the course's measurements, not this package's.

All statements here about `cmdstanr` behaviour were read from `cmdstanr` 0.9.0
under R 4.6.1, and none of them is a course measurement. They are: the
environment variable read by `cmdstanr:::cmdstanr_initialize()`, and its
fallback to `~/.cmdstan`; the resolution of a `CMDSTAN` directory that is not
itself an installation to the newest `cmdstan-*` inside it, and the absence of
that resolution from `set_cmdstan_path()`; the Rtools version returned by
`cmdstanr:::rtools4x_version()`; the text of three of the messages quoted above
(i.e. the toolchain success, the missing Rtools, and the directory assertion);
the assertion by `install_cmdstan()` that a supplied directory exists, instead
of creating it; the forwarding of `fix = TRUE` by `check_cmdstan_toolchain()` to
the branch that repairs problems instead of only reporting them; and the
`timeout` argument being passed to `build_cmdstan()` and `build_example()`,
while the download runs through `utils::download.file()` and so honours
`options(timeout)`.

The Linux route was checked on 2026-09-11 by running the course's verification
script end to end, with R 4.6.1, `brms` 2.23.0, `cmdstanr` 0.9.0 and CmdStan
2.39.0. This check has two limitations: it was run under WSL2 Debian and not on
a native installation, and the machine was already configured, so it confirms
that the end state works but does not test the instructions for reaching it.

